# .distignore: paths excluded from the distributed tarball.
# (Lives at the top of the tool folder, next to .gitignore.)
#
# The rules apply to the tool's own files. Dependencies fetched by
# scripts/get_libs.sh into libs/ ship as get_libs.sh left them; trim those in get_libs.sh.
#
# Syntax: EXACT .gitignore semantics (evaluated with `git check-ignore`).
# Paste .gitignore content verbatim: wildcards, dir-only `name/`, `!` negation,
# `/` anchoring, and `**` all work, at every depth of the tool folder. A
# whitelist (`*`, then `!*/` and the files to ship) works too: the build's own
# folders below never ship, whatever a `!` rule says.
# A rule without a `/`, or with only a trailing one, matches at every depth:
# `*.sh` would also drop src/tools/run.sh. A leading `/` anchors a rule to the
# top of the tool folder: `/Makefile` matches only the top-level Makefile.
#
# Built-in rules applied by scripts/core/build.sh (not listed here):
#   .*                       hidden files/dirs are excluded by default; LOWEST
#                            precedence, re-include with a `!` rule below
#   .git/  build/  .venv/  .ngargparser/
#                            the top-level git repository, build output,
#                            virtualenv and build state are ALWAYS excluded
#                            (a `!` rule cannot bring them back); so are
#                            venv/, env/ and .virtualenv/ when they hold a
#                            virtualenv (pyvenv.cfg), and any top-level
#                            folder that holds a conda environment
#                            (conda-meta/). The deploy host makes its own
#                            .venv with uv sync.
#   .git at any depth        a nested repository (a clone in src/, a
#                            submodule) is ALWAYS excluded too, since its
#                            config can hold a token; the build names one a
#                            `!` rule would ship in a notice.
#   *.ngargparser-restore  .<name>.<8 characters>.tmp  .<name>.<8 characters>.ngargparser-sync
#                            temporary files a stopped cli command (such as
#                            cli sync) left, at any depth, are ALWAYS
#                            excluded too; the build names them in a notice.
#   README  deploy/install.sh  .python-version
#                            ALWAYS included at the top level: the deploy
#                            orchestrator requires README and install.sh, and
#                            uv on the target host reads .python-version. A
#                            rule that would exclude one of them only prints a
#                            notice. Other files in deploy/ follow the rules here.
#
# The build fails when a rule here excludes pyproject.toml, uv.lock, configure,
# paths.py or a framework file in src/core/: deploy/install.sh needs them on
# the target host.
# `make build` lists what this file excluded; `make build-verbose` lists all of it.
#
# Re-including hidden things:
#   !.env                    ship a hidden file
#   !.streamlit/             ship a hidden dir (and everything inside it)
# To ship ONLY one file from a hidden dir, re-include the dir first (gitignore
# cannot re-include a file whose parent dir is excluded), then re-exclude the
# rest:
#   !.streamlit/
#   .streamlit/*
#   !.streamlit/config.toml
#
# This file is USER-OWNED: `cli sync` never overwrites it.

# Build-time files at the top of the tool folder: make build runs them, a deploy does not.
/Makefile
/scripts/
# Notes for the tool's developers; its users get README, which always ships. A
# tool whose pyproject.toml has a [build-system] and readme = "README.md" may
# need it on the deploy host (hatchling and uv_build read it there; setuptools
# only warns): delete this rule then.
/README.md
# Written on each host by ./configure.
/setup_*_env.sh
# The tool's own tests.
/tests/
__pycache__/
# Package metadata a build or an editable install writes.
*.egg-info/
*.bak
